# ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# DIC-1189 — Staging isolation (test.holohunter.dicoge.com)
# ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# APP_ENV / EXPO_PUBLIC_APP_ENV: single source of truth for staging vs
# production runtime. Fail-closed rule (src/config/appEnv.ts): any value that
# is not literally the trimmed lowercase string "staging" resolves to
# production. Unset / unknown / mistyped → production behaviour, so the
# staging affordances (TEST banner, robots noindex, `staging:` KV prefix) can
# only ever light up on the staging deployment. Only set these two on the
# holohunter-staging Vercel project — leave them blank in local dev unless you
# are simulating staging.
#   APP_ENV               — server-side (Node, Vercel functions)
#   EXPO_PUBLIC_APP_ENV   — client bundle (Expo baked at export time); set to
#                           the same value as APP_ENV on staging
# APP_ENV=staging
# EXPO_PUBLIC_APP_ENV=staging
#
# EXPO_PUBLIC_SITE_URL / SITE_URL — public origin for the deployment. Defaults
# to https://holohunter.dicoge.com (production) or
# https://test.holohunter.dicoge.com (staging). Only set explicitly if a
# custom host is needed (e.g. preview-branch deployment).
# EXPO_PUBLIC_SITE_URL=https://test.holohunter.dicoge.com
#
# EXPO_PUBLIC_STAGING_SHA — the git SHA baked into the staging bundle so the
# StagingBanner can display it and the /index.html <meta name="staging-sha">
# is auditable. On Vercel the workflow injects VERCEL_GIT_COMMIT_SHA into this
# name at buildCommand time; you almost never set it by hand.
# EXPO_PUBLIC_STAGING_SHA=
#
# ── Per-lane secrets (rework 3rd pass — blocker #3) ─────────────────────────
# Staging uses SEPARATE session and internal-webhook secrets from production
# so a leaked staging secret cannot forge production sessions/webhooks (and
# vice versa). session.ts / internal-auth.ts select the secret based on the
# strict server-side APP_ENV via resolveAppEnvStrict(); staging falls back to
# null (not the production secret) when the staging variant is unset — fail
# closed. The dic1189 setup workflow's `Verify staging server secrets are
# distinct from production` step refuses to apply if the staging value
# equals the production value byte-for-byte.
# AUTH_SESSION_SECRET_STAGING=            # staging session HMAC (mandatory; MUST differ from AUTH_SESSION_SECRET)
# PUSH_NOTIFY_SECRET_STAGING=             # staging internal-cron shared secret (mandatory; MUST differ from PUSH_NOTIFY_SECRET)
#
# ── Independent staging KV (operator-applied, rework 3rd pass — blocker #1) ─
# Automatic KV provisioning via /v1/storage/stores was sunset. The
# dic1189 workflow now REQUIRES the operator to pre-apply an independent
# Vercel KV / Upstash Redis instance via the Vercel Marketplace UI (or
# Upstash console) and register its REST url+token as GitHub secrets
# prefixed with STAGING_. The workflow verifies both are non-empty AND
# distinct from the production KV_REST_API_URL / KV_REST_API_TOKEN before
# writing them into the staging project. No fallback to shared production
# KV is permitted.
# STAGING_KV_REST_API_URL=                # applied staging KV REST URL — MUST differ from production
# STAGING_KV_REST_API_TOKEN=              # applied staging KV REST token — MUST differ from production
#
# ── Push notification staging guard (rework-blocker #3c) ────────────────────
# EXPO_STAGING_TEST_TOKENS — comma-separated allow-list of Expo push tokens
# that staging is allowed to send to. Empty / unset = staging cannot send to
# ANYONE (fail-closed default). Production ignores this list (its allow-list
# is the KV watchlist itself, which is keyed on production tokens).
# EXPO_STAGING_TEST_TOKENS=ExponentPushToken[synthetic-1],ExponentPushToken[synthetic-2]
#
# ── Payment env guard (api/_lib/env-guard.ts) ───────────────────────────────
# No live payment integration exists yet — the guard is scaffolding that fails
# closed the moment a payment secret shows up. Staging MUST reject live
# credentials, production MUST reject test credentials, unknown/missing env
# fails closed. assertPaymentEnv() is called at boot via the IIFE in
# api/_lib/kv-namespace.ts and MUST be called by any future Stripe /
# RevenueCat wiring before it constructs a provider client.
# STRIPE_SECRET_KEY=                      # sk_live_* on prod, sk_test_* on staging
# STRIPE_WEBHOOK_SECRET=                  # whsec_live_* on prod, whsec_test_* on staging
# EXPO_PUBLIC_STRIPE_PUBLISHABLE_KEY=     # pk_live_* on prod, pk_test_* on staging
# REVENUECAT_SECRET_KEY=                  # RC v3 secret; sk_live_* prod / sk_test_* staging
# EXPO_PUBLIC_REVENUECAT_IOS_KEY=         # appl_* live, sandbox_* test
# EXPO_PUBLIC_REVENUECAT_ANDROID_KEY=     # goog_* live, sandbox_* test
# STRIPE_MODE=                            # 'live' on prod / 'test' on staging (product-mode sentinel)
# REVENUECAT_ENVIRONMENT=                 # 'production' on prod / 'sandbox' on staging
# STRIPE_WEBHOOK_LIVEMODE=                # 'true' on prod / 'false' on staging (webhook livemode)
#
# ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Card recognition backend (scan + 相簿上傳).
#
# Google AI Studio (Generative Language API) key used by POST /api/recognize-card
# for Gemini Vision. Set it in the Vercel project for BOTH Preview and Production —
# it was undocumented here and consequently never provisioned, so every deployment
# answered 503 RECOGNITION_UNAVAILABLE and no photo could ever be recognised
# (DIC-1013 QA). It must be a Google AI Studio key (`AIza…`); an OpenRouter
# `sk-or-…` key is a different service and Gemini rejects it with 400.
# Unset → the endpoint stays fail-closed at 503 and the app tells the user the
# service is unavailable rather than blaming their photo.
GEMINI_API_KEY=

# Push notification backend (DIC-390)

# Vercel KV / Upstash Redis — stores push tokens, watchlists, and per-card
# alert cooldown timestamps. Replaces the old GitHub-committed JSON files so
# tokens never land in git history.
KV_REST_API_URL=
KV_REST_API_TOKEN=

# Shared secret guarding POST /api/push/notify. The endpoint is server-to-server
# only (cron / Vercel cron) and rejects every request when this is unset.
# Set the SAME value on Vercel (the API) and in the local scraper's environment.
PUSH_NOTIFY_SECRET=

# Optional override for where the scraper sends alerts (defaults to prod).
# PUSH_NOTIFY_URL=https://holocard-hunter.vercel.app/api/push/notify

# Google OAuth (all client IDs are PUBLIC values — the only Google secret is the
# Web client secret, which the app never uses and must never be committed).
#
# EXPO_PUBLIC_GOOGLE_WEB_CLIENT_ID — the Web/"server" OAuth client. Used by the
#   web browser sign-in AND as the `webClientId` for native Android Google
#   Sign-In (Credential Manager), so the Android ID token's `aud` is this Web
#   client — the audience the backend verifies. This is the id the server MUST
#   have to accept Android/web logins.
# EXPO_PUBLIC_GOOGLE_IOS_CLIENT_ID — dedicated iOS OAuth client (its own
#   reversed-client-id URL scheme) for native iOS Google Sign-In.
# EXPO_PUBLIC_GOOGLE_ANDROID_CLIENT_ID — dedicated Android OAuth client
#   (package `com.dicoge.holohunter` + signing-cert SHA-1). It AUTHORIZES the
#   native Android flow in Google Console; it is NOT passed to the app at
#   runtime (the library uses the Web client id above). The server accepts it as
#   an audience for defense-in-depth. Register a SHA-1 for every keystore that
#   signs the app — most importantly the Play App Signing key (see
#   docs/Auth-Google-Login-Design.md 交付 F).
EXPO_PUBLIC_GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=your-google-client-id.apps.googleusercontent.com
EXPO_PUBLIC_GOOGLE_WEB_CLIENT_ID=your-web-client-id.apps.googleusercontent.com
EXPO_PUBLIC_GOOGLE_IOS_CLIENT_ID=your-ios-client-id.apps.googleusercontent.com
EXPO_PUBLIC_GOOGLE_ANDROID_CLIENT_ID=your-android-client-id.apps.googleusercontent.com

# Sign in with Apple — Web + Android server-callback OAuth (DIC-960).
#
# iOS uses NATIVE Sign in with Apple and needs none of these. Web and Android run
# the server-callback path: the browser / Custom Tabs hit GET /api/auth/apple/web,
# Apple form_posts the id_token back to POST /api/auth/apple/web, and the app is
# deep-linked back with a server session. EVERY value below is fail-closed: if any
# is unset the endpoint returns 501 and the Apple button is hidden — never a
# nonfunctional button. Set the SECRET ones ONLY in Vercel env, never in git.
#
# APPLE_WEB_LOGIN_ENABLED — master gate; must be exactly "true" to enable web +
#   Android Apple (also gates the id_token audience allow-list in verify-token).
# APPLE_CLIENT_ID / EXPO_PUBLIC_APPLE_SERVICE_ID — the Apple Services ID (the
#   web/android OAuth client_id == id_token `aud`).
# APPLE_WEB_REDIRECT_URI — the HTTPS return URL registered with Apple that Apple
#   form_posts to; it must equal this endpoint's own URL, e.g.
#   https://holohunter.dicoge.com/api/auth/apple/web
# APPLE_WEB_ALLOWED_RETURNS — comma-separated allow-list of in-app return targets
#   the callback may bounce the browser back to (open-redirect defence). Include
#   the web SPA origin and the Android HTTPS App Link (NOT a custom scheme — a
#   custom scheme is not app-exclusive and could be intercepted, CR DIC-961), e.g.
#   https://holohunter.dicoge.com,https://holohunter.dicoge.com/auth/apple/return
# AUTH_SESSION_SECRET — already required for sessions; also signs the OAuth state.
# APPLE_WEB_LOGIN_ENABLED=true
# APPLE_CLIENT_ID=com.dicoge.holohunter.web
# APPLE_WEB_REDIRECT_URI=https://holohunter.dicoge.com/api/auth/apple/web
# APPLE_WEB_ALLOWED_RETURNS=https://holohunter.dicoge.com,https://holohunter.dicoge.com/auth/apple/return

# Android Apple App Link (DIC-960 / CR DIC-961). Android's return channel MUST be
# a VERIFIED HTTPS App Link. The callback never returns the session itself — it
# returns a one-time exchange code the app redeems (with a PKCE verifier it kept
# off the return channel) at POST /api/auth/apple-exchange. Android stays FAIL
# CLOSED (button hidden, no custom-scheme fallback) until BOTH of these are set:
#
# EXPO_PUBLIC_APPLE_ANDROID_ENABLED — client + build gate. Only set to "true"
#   AFTER assetlinks.json is deployed with the real fingerprint below; it turns on
#   the Android Apple button AND emits the autoVerify App Link intent filter.
# ANDROID_APP_LINK_SHA256 — server build-time only. Comma-separated SHA-256
#   signing-cert fingerprint(s) (upload key + Play App Signing key) used to
#   generate /.well-known/assetlinks.json. NEVER commit a real fingerprint; unset
#   → an empty (fail-closed) assetlinks.json that delegates to no app. Get it from
#   `keytool -list -v -keystore <keystore>` or Play Console → App signing.
# EXPO_PUBLIC_APPLE_ANDROID_APP_LINK_HOST — optional; App Link host (default
#   holohunter.dicoge.com). Must match APPLE_WEB_ALLOWED_RETURNS + assetlinks host.
# EXPO_PUBLIC_APPLE_ANDROID_RETURN_URL — optional; full App Link the app returns
#   to (default https://holohunter.dicoge.com/auth/apple/return).
# NOTE: the assetlinks delegation package is HARD-CODED to com.dicoge.holohunter
#   and is deliberately NOT operator-configurable — an override would let build
#   access delegate the HoloHunter host to another package (CR DIC-961).
# EXPO_PUBLIC_APPLE_ANDROID_ENABLED=true
# ANDROID_APP_LINK_SHA256=14:6D:E9:...:AB   # real fingerprint — set in CI/EAS env only

# Store MVP release gate (DIC-908). Allowlist / fail-closed: hides advanced
# features (buy-back price, price spread, trend & prediction, news, YouTube
# stats, watchlist alerts, subscription) from the store build.
#   1 / true  → Store MVP ON  (hide advanced surfaces)
#   0 / false → Store MVP OFF (full app)
#   unset     → native store builds default ON (fail-closed); web defaults OFF
#               so the existing web production site keeps its full feature set.
# eas.json's preview/production profiles already pin this to "1".
# EXPO_PUBLIC_STORE_MVP=1
